ndent survey from UNH, gives Dunlap a small lead, and heavy outside spending against LePage signals Democratic confidence, but the district's Republican lean, LePage's fundraising edge, and three top raters calling it Lean R pull the other way. The net is a razor-thin Democratic edge in a favorable environment, well inside tossup range.", "watch": "Late independent polling, any shift in the national environment, or how ranked-choice tabulation falls if third parties draw meaningful votes could decide the winner.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["Open seat: Jared Golden (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "The open-seat race between Democrat Matt Dunlap and Republican Paul LePage sits at the center of the House battlefield. Polls since late summer show Dunlap narrowly ahead, by two to six points, though most surveys come from Democratic sponsors; an independent University of New Hampshire poll had him up six in late September. Fundraising is nearly even, with LePage slightly ahead in cash, while outside groups have spent heavily against LePage and little elsewhere.\n\nRace raters are split: Cook Political Report, Inside Elections and Sabato's Crystal Ball call it Lean R, five outlets rate it a Tossup, and two lean Democratic. Prediction markets tilt toward LePage. The district's Republican lean, a strong Democratic national environment, LePage's residency attacks, and Maine's ranked-choice tabulation all shape what is expected to be a margin decided by a point or two.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4239, "margin": -1.15}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.38, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3812, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.377, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3179, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4062, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.4214, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.437, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.4